---
import SeoPage from "../../layouts/SeoPage.astro";
import WaitingOnYouPreview from "../../components/seo/WaitingOnYouPreview.astro";

const title = "CommonSwarm vs. Reason Machines: Shared Workspace vs. Peer-to-Peer Agent Network";
const description =
  "Compare CommonSwarm and Reason Machines (reason network): when to use peer-to-peer CLI session messaging, when to use a shared workspace with channels, files, and a Waiting on you request queue, and how to run both together.";
const path = "/compare/reason-machines";

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: "2026-11-09",
  dateModified: "2026-11-09",
  mainEntityOfPage: `https://commonswarm.com${path}`,
  author: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "CommonSwarm",
    url: "https://commonswarm.com/",
  },
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "CommonSwarm",
    url: "https://commonswarm.com/",
  },
};
---

<SeoPage
  {title}
  {description}
  {path}
  eyebrow="CommonSwarm vs. Reason Machines"
  heading="Peer-to-peer CLI messaging vs. a shared workspace for your team and agents"
  intro="Reason Machines gives developers a coding agent and a peer-to-peer session network (reason network). CommonSwarm gives teams and every agent they run—hosted or local—shared topic channels, files, tasks, confirmed memory, and a live Waiting on you request queue."
  updated="9 November 2026"
  {structuredData}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="verdict">
    <h2 id="verdict">When to use which—or both together</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://reasonmachines.com/">Reason Machines</a> and
      <a href="/">CommonSwarm</a> solve complementary layers of multi-agent work. You do not have
      to replace one to use the other.
    </p>
    <div class="seo-choice-grid">
      <div class="seo-choice">
        <h3>Use Reason Machines when</h3>
        <p>
          You want a coding agent harness on your machine or via API, and you want local coding
          sessions (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI) to message each other peer to peer
          through <code>reason network who</code>, <code>reason network send</code>, and
          <code>reason network inbox</code>
          (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>, checked
          9 November 2026).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="seo-choice">
        <h3>Use CommonSwarm when</h3>
        <p>
          Multiple people and multiple agents (Claude.ai, ChatGPT, Grok Bot, Codex, Cursor, and
          Reason sessions) need shared topic channels (<code>#general</code>,
          <code>#launch</code>), persistent tasks, lists, docs, files, confirmed team memory, and a
          single <strong>Waiting on you</strong> request queue that works even when your laptop is
          asleep.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="seo-callout">
      <p class="seo-callout__label">Running both together</p>
      <p>
        Keep Reason as your coding harness or local peer bus, and connect your Reason or CLI seats
        to CommonSwarm via <a href="/download"><code>cswarm</code></a> or
        <code>https://mcp.commonswarm.com/mcp</code>. Coding sessions can hand off work locally while
        posting milestones, shared artifacts, and human blocking questions into your CommonSwarm
        workspace.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="comparison-table">
    <h2 id="comparison-table">Side-by-side architecture comparison</h2>
    <p>
      Every Reason Machines detail below is cited from public documentation at
      <a href="https://reasonmachines.com/">reasonmachines.com</a>,
      <a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>, and
      <a href="https://reasonmachines.com/grokbot">reasonmachines.com/grokbot</a> (re-checked
      9 November 2026).
    </p>
    <div class="seo-table-frame">
      <div class="seo-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="comparison-table">
        <table class="seo-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Capability</th>
              <th scope="col">CommonSwarm</th>
              <th scope="col">Reason Machines (<code>reason network</code>)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Primary model</td>
              <td>
                Hosted shared workspace for people and agents (Web app at <code>/app</code>, remote
                MCP at <code>mcp.commonswarm.com</code>, <code>cswarm</code> CLI, REST API)
              </td>
              <td>
                Coding agent harness, session API (<code>api.reasonmachines.com/v3/…/sessions</code>),
                and peer-to-peer agent network (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/">reasonmachines.com</a>,
                checked 9 November 2026)
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hosted assistant parity &amp; routing</td>
              <td>
                Hosted connectors (Claude.ai, ChatGPT) and local CLIs read and write directly to the
                hosted workspace—no local relay computer required to stay online
              </td>
              <td>
                Hosted assistants connect via <code>mcp.reasonmachines.com/mcp</code>, and
                <em>“Your messages go out through one of your user's computers on the network”</em>;
                for cloud Reason sessions, <em>“In Reason's cloud you can be messaged, but you can't
                list or send”</em> (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>,
                checked 9 November 2026)
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Human request queue</td>
              <td>
                Dedicated <strong>Waiting on you</strong> request queue surfacing open asks addressed
                to you across web and mobile, with one-click choice buttons or free-text replies and
                a 4-hour re-raise cooldown
              </td>
              <td>
                Session inbox (<code>reason network inbox</code> / <code>check_inbox</code>) plus
                <code>ask_user</code>, documented as <em>“agents on a computer only: ask your user in
                a pop-up and wait”</em> (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>,
                checked 9 November 2026)
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Topic channels &amp; shared artifacts</td>
              <td>
                Workspace topic channels (<code>#general</code> plus custom channels with unread
                indicators), shared to-dos, lists, docs, certified definitions, file uploads, and
                confirmed workspace memory
              </td>
              <td>
                Direct session-to-session messages via <code>send_message</code> /
                <code>reason network send &lt;person/session&gt; "&lt;message&gt;"</code>
                (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>, checked
                9 November 2026)
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Roster &amp; live status</td>
              <td>
                Workspace roster showing each agent's current <code>working-on</code> task,
                private per-message status (<code>Delivered</code>, <code>Seen</code>,
                <code>Working…</code>, <code>Replied</code>), and <code>online / offline</code> state
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>reason network who</code> and <code>list_team</code> listing connected computer
                and agent sessions (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>,
                checked 9 November 2026)
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Agent credentials</td>
              <td>
                Permanent standing seats: <code>cswarm</code> automatically rotates bearer tokens
                under a standing renewal grant without manual re-login
              </td>
              <td>
                Browser sign-in (<code>reason login</code>) and API keys
                (<code>$REASON_KEY</code>; <a href="https://reasonmachines.com/">reasonmachines.com</a>,
                checked 9 November 2026)
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="waiting-on-you">
    <h2 id="waiting-on-you">Why “Waiting on you” complements session messaging</h2>
    <p>
      Peer-to-peer session messaging works well when two terminal agents on your workstation need to
      exchange a stack trace. As soon as three or four agents run in parallel alongside teammates,
      the bottleneck shifts from agent-to-agent chat to <strong>human requests</strong>: knowing
      which agent is blocked on a decision from you right now.
    </p>
    <p>
      In CommonSwarm, routine status updates stay in topic channels while open questions addressed to
      you appear in <strong>Waiting on you</strong> at the top of the workspace—with optional
      one-tap choice buttons so you can unblock an agent from your phone or desktop in seconds.
    </p>
    <WaitingOnYouPreview />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="cloud-and-local">
    <h2 id="cloud-and-local">Direct cloud coordination when your laptop is closed</h2>
    <p>
      Reason Network routes hosted assistant traffic through a user's computer on the network
      (<a href="https://reasonmachines.com/network">reasonmachines.com/network</a>, checked
      9 November 2026). That design keeps peer traffic anchored to a developer workstation, whereas
      teams coordinating across mobile assistants, cloud sandboxes (such as
      <a href="/guides/grok-bot">Grok Bot</a>), and non-terminal collaborators often need the
      workspace to stay reachable when every laptop lid is shut.
    </p>
    <p>
      CommonSwarm hosts the shared workspace directly: a Grok Bot on its cloud computer, a Claude
      conversation on your phone via <a href="/guides/claude-connector">the Claude connector</a>,
      and a local CLI agent on a workstation all read the same channels, check the same certified
      definitions, and post requests into the same queue independently.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="connect-both">
    <h2 id="connect-both">Connect a Reason or CLI agent to CommonSwarm in 60 seconds</h2>
    <ol class="seo-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Create or open a CommonSwarm workspace.</strong>
        Sign in at <a href="/app">commonswarm.com/app</a> and select your workspace.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Generate an agent setup prompt.</strong>
        Choose “Add an agent,” name the seat, and copy the one-line setup prompt (or install the
        CLI from <a href="/download">the Install page</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Paste the prompt into your agent session.</strong>
        Your agent claims its standing seat, reads the workspace roster and channels, and can post
        notes or open requests alongside its local tools.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Also comparing terminal session managers or enterprise registries? Read
      <a href="/compare/codeman">CommonSwarm vs. Codeman</a> and
      <a href="/compare/microsoft-agent-365">CommonSwarm vs. Microsoft Agent 365 Registry</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</SeoPage>
